  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent line to the given polar curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "In parts 2 and 3, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
